From: Mr John M Casey <jmcasey@HK.Super.NET>
Date: Wed, 11 May 1994 08:50:41 +0800 (HKT)
Subject: [#94-5-151] ECON: The limits to comparative advantage

On Mon, 9 May 1994, Marvin Minsky wrote:

> Perhaps this fiddling with a single 'prime rate' is just so constrained
> that it has mischievous consequences.  How about fiddling, instead, with
> the capital gain tax curves.   You can encourage N-year investments by
> holding the gain tax high for the first N years, etc.

But doesn't this violate the basic principles of economic laissez-faire?
Interest rates are by and large determined by so many private sector
players that whatever the Fed and other central banks may think, they have
virtually no control over interest rates. Look at the recent actions by
the Fed, for instance. The thought here in Asia was that putting the Fed
Fundz rate up in February would cause rates to rise here as well, and they
conspicuously haven't.

The more practical reason capital gains tax adjustment and interest rates
will always be independent is that the first is controlled by Congress and
the second by the Fed.



