On Tue, 10 May 1994, Alexander Chislenko wrote:

>   One could use a large system of differential equations as a generic model
> here, but I doubt that any description consisting of a small combination
> of words can have more than educational value.

Actually, I sort of do that very thing for a living. The trick isn't to
explain it adequately, so much as to be able mostly to predict within a
reasonable margin of error the likelihood of certain events having causal
relationships. Chaos theory is actually quite handy.

>   Economists have no idea how to calculate inflation and productivity in
> the new areas/products - the heart of modern economic development -... 
> so they just ignore all of them, and calculate their factors looking
> at conventional industries.   And then they regulate the system they
> don't understand: when the real growth rate exceeds their expectations,
> the Federal Reserve tries to cool/choke off the 'overheated' economy by
> raising interest rates...

Absurd, innit? China is the best example of this, because the govt has SO
little control over the marketplace until it's SO late they just end up
amplifying the problem -- by the point the policy takes effect the market
has started cooling off anyway.

> since future [supposedly skyroketing] *real* interest rates will be
> determined by the growth of the productive value of capital, while
> the *nominal* rates will depend on monetary factors, we may see
> a 100% real rate turn into %50 deflation with the nominal interest
> (taxed by the government) close to 0. 

Y'know, Hong Kong real interest rates have been negative for years.
Nominals are still positive, but with the HK$ pegged to the US$ (for the
time being) this is one of the best rackets in the world. I bring this up
because it's a great example of productive value of capital in a
hypergrowth phase (see China).




