From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)
Date: Tue, 10 May 1994 21:46:05 -0500 (CDT)
Subject: [#94-5-153] ECON: The limits to comparative advantage

Mr. John M. Casey:

> There's a book by Roger Penrose called The Emperor's New Mind, that
> discusses in painstaking detail the possibility of computers (incl robots)
> ever being able to truly make the leaps of logic that can enable a human
> to, for instance, prove or disprove a theorem.

Not at all.  I never cease to be amazed that anybody takes Penrose's
book to be anything at all relevant to any discussion about the
abilities of computers.  My somewhat careful reading of his
text doesn't reveal anything more than a transparent restatement
of Lucas's misguided application of Godel's Theorem to the "problem"
of consciousness.

I can write a program that will claim in English text anything
you'd like; in this way it resembles the "leaps of logic" that
anthropocentric writers like Penrose and Searle like to trumpet as
some uniquely "human" ability.  But really it's nothing more than
the difference between induction and deduction.  As a cognitive
scientist, Penrose is a wonderful physicist (and his presentation
of phase space is quite delectable) -- but vague talk about
hypothetical quantum powers of nervous systems is just that:
vague talk.

Please enlighten us, even breifly, about any specific point Penrose
makes regarding the limits of computers that is non-silly.  What
human mathematicians call "proof" is most often a social process
and must be judged as such.

Were you a member of the Accountability Society (a relic of a bygone
era on this list, alas), I'd make this request a formal Challenge.
As a start, I refer you to Hans Moravec's very lucid reply to Penrose.

derek
